//RS //! Streaming compiler trace.
//RS //!
//RS //! `JET_TRACE_FILE=<path>` makes the compiler write Chrome trace-event JSON
//RS //! to `<path>` while it runs: one event per line, flushed per event, so a
//RS //! running or killed compile is still a loadable trace (Perfetto and
//RS //! chrome://tracing accept the JSON array without its closing `]`).
//RS //! `JET_TRACE_DETAIL=functions` also asks for one span per function.
//RS //!
//RS //! This is the one trace writer. The Rust driver opens spans with [`span`];
//RS //! the bootstrap host hands the same sink to the Jet compiler as its
//RS //! `CompilerTraceSink`. Unset, every span site costs one check of the
//RS //! process-wide sink: nothing is formatted or allocated.
//RS //!
//RS //! Spans nest per thread. A span's `kind` is its stable event category
//RS //! (`load`, `sema.check`, ...); `detail` labels one item (a module or a
//RS //! function) and becomes the label the viewer shows. `end(kind)` closes the
//RS //! innermost open span of that kind together with any deeper span an early
//RS //! return left open, and records the items processed and the process's
//RS //! resident and peak memory (`VmRSS`, `VmHWM`).
//RS 
